      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The narrowest dimension of the adult female birth canal (obstetric conjugate) is roughly 110 mm, the transverse diameter of the pelvic inlet is about 130 mm, and the transverse diameter of the pelvic outlet is about 120 mm.",
      "evidence": "Obstetrics texts (e.g., Cunningham et al., 2014) give typical obstetric conjugate ≈ 11 cm and transverse inlet/outlet ≈ 12–13 cm in a normal pelvis.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The skull’s greatest linear dimension (≈ 170 mm) exceeds the smallest pelvic dimension (≈ 110 mm) by roughly 60 mm.",
      "evidence": "Direct subtraction of the measurements from steps 1 and 2.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Adult cranial bones are fully fused cortical bone that cannot be compressed enough to reduce their size without fracturing.",
      "evidence": "Biomechanical data show cortical bone yields at ≈ 130 MPa; compressing a 140 mm skull to < 110 mm would require forces far beyond physiological limits and would cause fracture.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Because the skull is larger than the birth canal and cannot be deformed sufficiently, an intact adult skull cannot pass through the birth canal.",
      "evidence": "Logical combination of steps 3 (size mismatch) and 4 (lack of deformability).",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    }
